## SMTP settings, required for e.g. sign-up
## Transport security is `entrystore.smtp.security`. This file documented it as `entrystore.smtp.ssl`,
## a key the code never read, up to and including 5.6.2 — deployments configured from one of those
## copies were therefore sending plaintext. 5.7.0 through 5.8.0 documented the canonical key
## correctly. As of 6.1 both spellings are honoured: `entrystore.smtp.security` is read whenever it
## resolves, and `entrystore.smtp.ssl` only as a fallback. Setting both is allowed; if they disagree,
## `security` applies and the deprecated key is reported at WARN. Values are matched on letters and
## digits only, case-insensitively, so `STARTTLS`, `start-tls` and the `START_TLS` form an environment
## variable naturally carries all resolve alike.
## Neither key was validated before 6.1 — `ssl` was never read, and `security` was compared with
## equalsIgnoreCase — so a value outside starttls|ssl|off silently meant plaintext. Such a value is now
## reported at ERROR and NO MAIL IS SENT while it stays set: sending in the clear under a configuration
## meant to be encrypted is not a safe default. Startup is never aborted over it — a mail
## misconfiguration must not take the REST API down. A configured host that ends up on plaintext
## without `security` being set at all is warned about at startup, so a missing setting is loud rather
## than silent; setting `entrystore.smtp.security=off` declares plaintext and silences that warning.
## `entrystore.smtp.ssl` is deprecated and will be removed in a future major version.
##
## UPGRADE NOTE: because `entrystore.smtp.ssl` is now honoured and an unresolvable value now refuses to
## send, transport security is enforced rather than downgraded, and a deployment carrying a stale value
## in either key will see sends start FAILING rather than continuing insecurely:
##   - `entrystore.smtp.ssl=ssl` with the `entrystore.smtp.port=587` this file documented alongside it
##     means implicit TLS on a submission port, so every send fails.
##   - `entrystore.smtp.ssl=starttls` against a relay that does not advertise STARTTLS now throws
##     instead of silently downgrading.
##   - a value neither key can resolve — `security=tls`, `=none`, `=true` — refuses to send at all.
## Sign-up, password reset, POST /message and the password-change confirmations stop working in each of
## those cases. Verify your MTA supports the implied mode before upgrading, and declare a deliberately
## plaintext relay explicitly with `entrystore.smtp.security=off`.
# entrystore.smtp.host=smtp.host.tld
# entrystore.smtp.port=587 (default: 25)
# entrystore.smtp.security=starttls|ssl|off (default: off)
## Both credential keys must be set. If only one is, that is reported at ERROR and SMTP
## authentication is disabled — the session stays anonymous, as before 6.1 — rather than failing
## startup.
# entrystore.smtp.username=smtp-user
# entrystore.smtp.password=smtp-password
## Envelope addresses. These supersede entrystore.auth.email.from|bcc, which are still read as a
## fallback and log a deprecation warning at startup when used. A value that is not a valid address is
## reported at ERROR at startup, naming the key. bcc and reply-to may each be a comma-separated list —
## legal for Reply-To under RFC 5322 and accepted here since 2019 — while from must be exactly one
## mailbox. The recipient and the Reply-To of POST /message are caller-supplied rather than configured
## here, and must each be exactly one mailbox: no list, no RFC822 group address and no CR or LF.
# entrystore.smtp.email.from=signup@yourdomain.tld
# entrystore.smtp.email.bcc=info+signup@yourdomain.tld
# entrystore.smtp.email.reply-to=support@yourdomain.tld
## Socket timeouts and TLS server-identity checking, hardcoded before 6.1. The defaults below are the
## values that were previously compiled in; lower them only if your MTA is reliably fast.
## Disabling check-server-identity makes TLS vulnerable to an active man-in-the-middle — it exists for
## test MTAs with self-signed certificates, not for production.
# entrystore.smtp.connection-timeout=5s
# entrystore.smtp.read-timeout=5s
# entrystore.smtp.write-timeout=5s
# entrystore.smtp.check-server-identity=true
## TOTAL number of send attempts before giving up, not the number of retries: 3 means two retries and
## 1 disables retrying. Must be between 1 and 10. Attempts are immediate (no backoff), so a single send
## can occupy its thread for this many times the whole SMTP exchange. Note that read-timeout above
## bounds each individual read, not the exchange, and one exchange makes seven or so blocking reads
## (greeting, EHLO, MAIL FROM, RCPT TO, DATA, end-of-data, QUIT) — plus DNS resolution, which no
## timeout covers. Which thread depends on the path: sign-up, POST /message, the /auth/pwreset
## confirmation and a user password change all send on the request thread, while password-reset mail
## runs on the passwordResetExecutor (2 threads, queue of 100, abort policy), so raising this value
## there fills that queue until dispatches are rejected rather than slowing individual requests.
## A permanent failure is not retried at all — a message that will not parse, an address the server
## rejected, or an authentication failure the server did not report with a 4xx code. A message the
## transport reports as partially delivered is never re-sent, so that path cannot produce a duplicate
## password-reset link; a failure that reports no delivered addresses at all, such as a read timeout
## after DATA, is still retried and can. The startup log warns when the timeouts and this value multiply
## out to a long block.
# entrystore.smtp.max-send-attempts=3
##
## Note: the spring.mail.* keys are not used; EntryStore builds its mail sender from the
## entrystore.smtp.* keys above. spring.mail.test-connection currently has no effect, because Spring
## Boot's validator requires a single JavaMailSenderImpl bean and EntryStore declares the bean as the
## JavaMailSender interface. Keep that declared type — narrowing it would make the flag open an SMTP
## connection during startup.

