## Multipart upload limits at the Spring servlet layer (defaults: 100MB).
## Application-level caps that DO exist: entrystore.data.max-file-size
## (resource uploads), entrystore.echo.max-file-size for /echo. POST /{context-id}/import
## (multipart variant) has NO application-level cap, so its upper bound is
## whatever you set here. The non-multipart byte[] variant of /import
## currently has no enforced server-side size cap (Spring's multipart caps
## do not apply to @RequestBody byte[], and the configured
## server.jetty.max-http-form-post-size only bounds form-urlencoded bodies)
## and should be fronted by a reverse-proxy body limit if exposed externally.
##
## Rejection contract: Jetty 12 enforces these caps at the container layer
## and rejects oversize multipart requests with status 400 ("bad multipart")
## BEFORE the Spring DispatcherServlet sees the request, so callers should
## treat a 400 on a multipart upload as a possible cap violation rather than
## necessarily a malformed body.
##
## DoS surface to consider before raising these: any authenticated user with
## WriteResource ACL on a target entry can open parallel slow PUTs, each
## spooled to spring.servlet.multipart.location (java.io.tmpdir by default,
## often tmpfs/RAM on modern distros). entrystore.data.max-file-size defaults
## to -1 (no application backstop), so the servlet-layer cap here is the
## effective per-request ceiling unless an operator opts into a tighter cap.
## Use -1B to disable the servlet-layer cap entirely (unlimited).
# spring.servlet.multipart.max-file-size=100MB
# spring.servlet.multipart.max-request-size=100MB

