## SSRF whitelist for the resource-DELETE proxy path
## (DELETE /{context}/resource/{id}?proxy=true on Link/Reference/LinkReference entries).
## Entries are ORIGIN URLs (scheme://host:port), NOT bare hostnames — this differs from
## the proxy-GET whitelist `entrystore.proxy.whitelist.local`, which is host-only.
## http://x normalizes to http://x:80 and https://x to https://x:443. The origin of
## entrystore.rowstore.url above (when set) is implicitly trusted for the DELETE path,
## so single-host RowStore deployments need no additional configuration here.
## Use the indexed form (.1, .2, ...); a bare "whitelist=origin" value is not supported.
# entrystore.proxy.remote-resource.delete.whitelist.1=http://rowstore.internal:8282
# entrystore.proxy.remote-resource.delete.whitelist.2=https://other-rowstore.example.com

## SSRF whitelist for the proxy-GET path. Entries are bare hostnames, not origin URLs.
## A listed host is exempt from the SSRF hostname blacklist AND from the loopback,
## site-local and link-local checks on the address it resolves to, so list only hosts
## that are meant to be reachable from the server.
## Use the indexed form (.1, .2, ...); a bare "local=host" value is not supported.
# entrystore.proxy.whitelist.local.1=cache.internal
# entrystore.proxy.whitelist.local.2=metadata.internal

## Hosts that guest (unauthenticated) users may proxy to. Requests to any other host
## require authentication. Bare hostnames, matched case-insensitively.
## Use the indexed form (.1, .2, ...); a bare "anonymous=host" value is not supported.
# entrystore.proxy.whitelist.anonymous.1=public.example.com

## Outbound-fetch limits, hardcoded before 6.1. The defaults below are the values that were
## previously compiled in. max-redirects, connect-timeout and read-timeout apply to BOTH outbound
## paths: GET /proxy (and its context-scoped form) and the resource-DELETE proxy path above.
## max-response-size applies to GET /proxy only — the resource-DELETE path never reads a response body.
##
## max-redirects is a cap on redirect hops, so a value of N opens up to N+1 connections (the initial
## request plus N hops); 0 disables redirect following entirely. Exceeding it is answered 502.
##
## The timeouts apply per hop. Establishing all hops costs at worst roughly
## (max-redirects + 1) x connect-timeout. read-timeout is a per-socket-read deadline that resets on
## every successful read, NOT a deadline for the response, so it does not bound total wall time: an
## upstream that trickles bytes just under the timeout holds a request thread until max-response-size
## is reached. DNS resolution is covered by no timeout here at all. The proxy runs on the request
## thread, so if the concern is threads held by a slow upstream, lower max-response-size — lowering
## the timeouts alone does not create a bound.
##
## max-response-size caps the upstream body that GET /proxy will buffer; exceeding it is answered 502.
## The body is accumulated in memory, so this bounds heap per concurrent proxy request. Capped at
## 512MB, because the buffer is an int-indexed array: without that ceiling a large enough value would
## exhaust the heap before the size check could fire.
# entrystore.proxy.max-response-size=10MB
# entrystore.proxy.max-redirects=15
# entrystore.proxy.connect-timeout=30s
# entrystore.proxy.read-timeout=60s

## Maximum payload POST /echo will reflect back; exceeding it is answered 413. Reported as
## echoMaxEntitySize by GET /management/status/extended. Keep
## spring.servlet.multipart.max-file-size at or above this value, otherwise Jetty rejects the upload
## with a 400 before /echo ever sees it. Capped at 64MB: the payload is read back as a string and
## HTML-escaped, so peak heap is a multiple of this value on an endpoint that needs no authentication.
# entrystore.echo.max-file-size=10MB

